00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:31.420
It's fine. You usually take, if you're not, if you're not timed, you usually take some good time between moves anyways.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:34.420
But when I'm playing against the computer, sometimes I just want to keep things moving.

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:37.940
It's just, like, the game does not want that to happen.

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:50.940
Okay, in 1993, which really isn't too far away from 1990, uh, Star Wars chess is gonna come out on, um, computers and Sega CD and that's probably about it.

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:52.740
But, like, that, that's gonna come out.

00:09:52.740 --> 00:10:13.200
And I think, even though that's just the same normal chess, right, except for now Luke Skywalker and Darth Vader can go toe-to-toe instead of two random kings you've never heard of, um, I think with the appeal there works a little bit more because you are, you have established characters that you kind of want to see, like, everybody's animation for.

00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:19.460
I want to see how Luke Skywalker defeats every single one of the Empire's pawns, etc., you know?

