To, to Sean's point, if it was more like Archon and it kind of became, like, a, uh, 1v1 fight where you at least maybe had, I don't know, not stats, but, like, obviously the knight should be more powerful, but if you can out-time him or something, the pawn could potentially take back the move.

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:18.520
That would run some real risk implications to every single thing that you do, because if, uh, if you put your rook up against a pawn and there's not a 100% guaranteed chance of capture, you might second-think those moves and it might make the chess game a little more, uh, strategic.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:42.620
Yeah, and just to, to further develop this game, this imaginary chess game that the three of us are making right now, I, I think you'd have to also have, not, not only, like, oh yeah, of course the, you know, the knight is, is stronger than a pawn, but you'd also have to have an advantage if you are, if you're in a position where in regular chess you would take the other thing.

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:49.780
Like, if a pawn is about to take a knight in regular chess, that should give you something to make it still play like regular chess, you know?

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:54.580
