Or is it just, like, a Sega CD?

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:41.900
But, I don't know. I didn't research that part.

00:17:42.080 --> 00:17:51.520
But the battle chess version for the Amiga CD TV, uh, added fully voiced introductions describing, uh, the movements of the different pieces.

00:17:51.980 --> 00:17:55.580
So that there was at least some kind of tutorialization of the game.

00:17:55.580 --> 00:18:00.480
But didn't you think it was gonna be fully voiced, like, completely when I was reading that?

00:18:00.620 --> 00:18:03.960
Like, not just, like, oh, we added an intro that explains how the game works.

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:05.780
And then we left the game as is.

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:10.720
Wait, like, so there's, like, there's dialogue?

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:12.540
Like, the pieces talk to each other now?

00:18:12.940 --> 00:18:14.280
There's no. See, that's what I'm saying.

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:16.040
Didn't you think that's what I was getting to?

00:18:16.040 --> 00:18:24.040
Yeah. Instead, it's just, like, we added this thing before you start playing battle chess that explains how the pieces in chess move.

00:18:24.280 --> 00:18:27.820
Wow. Well, okay, but that is something I thought was kind of weird.

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:30.200
And this is, like, this is, like, getting granular now.

00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:32.100
But you boot up battle chess.

00:18:32.480 --> 00:18:37.280
And, like, for me, I didn't even get to the mint, to, like, the, the, not even menu, the title screen.

00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:39.700
It was just, like, the copyright information.

00:18:39.700 --> 00:18:42.140
And then I, like, pressed A or start or whatever.

00:18:42.340 --> 00:18:44.260
And then it's, like, the game had started.

00:18:45.160 --> 00:18:47.520
There's no menu. You don't select your mode or anything.

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:49.140
There's no press start to begin.

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:50.900
Which is fine. It was just weird to me.

00:18:50.900 --> 00:18:53.480
It just felt, like, it felt kind of just thrown in there.

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:57.260
Because there was no menu or anything, I was, like, is this game finished?

00:18:57.560 --> 00:18:58.820
Yes, that is true.

00:18:58.820 --> 00:19:05.440
Because you're right. You have to then menu through that first game that it sets up for you to change anything about it.

00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:06.500
Which just feels awkward.

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:14.040
You should always, according to every NES game we've ever played, you should always set up everything first and then go into the game.

00:19:14.220 --> 00:19:29.380
Right. I think it would have been more on brand if it gave you a blank, a blank chessboard and then each, like, like, like, like coming out onto a stage, each piece slowly trudges to its spot.

00:19:29.620 --> 00:19:35.660
Yeah. Like, I mean, they do that in Arkham, but it's, but it's fast and cool.

00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:37.160
And you can skip it, I think.

00:19:37.160 --> 00:19:46.980
Mm-hmm. In 1991, a year later after this release, Battle Chess Enhanced was released for the PC and Mac.

00:19:47.280 --> 00:19:51.140
And it really is just updated graphics.

00:19:51.140 --> 00:19:54.660
Most of even the battle animations remain the same.

00:19:54.820 --> 00:20:04.820
And they didn't even fix the speed or, you know, the walking speed of the characters or anything or their specific looks or whatever.

00:20:04.820 --> 00:20:06.840
It's just, like, hey, nicer graphics.

00:20:07.160 --> 00:20:09.660
So I guess that's all you could expect in a year's time.

00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:12.540
But does that deserve the enhanced moniker?

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:14.040
I guess it's not.

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:15.500
I guess it's describing it.

00:20:16.260 --> 00:20:20.320
